      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood occupies the oldest part of Madrid, the Islamic citadel that once lay inside the city walls.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrio_de_La_Latina_(Madrid)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Plaza de la Cebada has served as a wholesale food market since the fifteenth century, a tradition that began outside the Puerta de Moros.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrio_de_La_Latina_(Madrid)",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "San Pedro el Viejo Church houses a 16th‑century chapel founded by Francisco Luján, located in the left nave’s chancel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.esmadrid.com/en/madrid-neighbourhoods/latina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "La Latina is famous for its weekly El Rastro flea market, tapas bars, the La Paloma festival, and the nearby Las Vistillas Gardens.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.esmadrid.com/en/madrid-neighbourhoods/latina",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The neighborhood sits in Madrid’s historic center, bordered by landmarks such as the Royal Palace, Plaza Mayor, and the Almudena Cathedral.",
